module v2.workflow.floor_expected_red

import v2.std.algebra { Cons, Empty, filter, list_flat_map }
import v2.std.collection { List }
import v2.std.text { String }

// 101 ROWS LEFT THIS ROSTER ON 2026-08-20 AND WERE NEVER ELIGIBLE FOR IT, which is a fact about
// this mechanism rather than about those rows, so it is recorded at the top where the next
// author enrolling something will read it.
//
// They were reclassified into `v2.workflow.floor_route_gap`. Every one of them was already
// executing every run, already reaching a HERMETIC REFUSAL — a host effect the hermetic route
// has no arm for — and because that refusal arrived as a `TypeError` carrying prose rather than
// as a typed outcome, `ExpectedRedArm` could only read it as an ordinary failure and HELD it as
// agreement. So this roster asserted "this witness runs and fails, and someone is fixing it"
// about 101 identities that NEVER REACHED THEIR SUBJECT. Nothing was fixing them, because there
// was nothing to fix: they needed a ROUTE, not a REPAIR.
//
// THE ARITHMETIC IS WHAT MAKES IT A FINDING RATHER THAN A SUSPICION. Floor run 32345970386
// carried 308 enrolled identities and held 207; the 101 difference is exactly the set of
// enrolled identities that route-gapped. Not approximately — the same set.
//
// THE LESSON FOR THIS ROSTER, and it is the one this header did not previously state: ENROLLING
// AN IDENTITY ASSERTS THAT IT REACHES ITS SUBJECT AND ANSWERS. A row that cannot run is not
// debt this roster can carry, and it is the more dangerous mistake, because a genuine red is
// loud when someone finally looks at it while a never-executed row looks identical to progress.
// `ExpectedRedArm` now refuses to hold `HostEffectRefused`, `HostToolUnresolved`, and an
// interrupted budget outcome for exactly this reason — three ways of producing no verdict, none
// of them agreement.
//
// THE EXPECTED-RED ROSTER: identities this branch KNOWS fail, enrolled by exact qualified name.
//
// This is NOT a skip list and the distinction is the whole justification for it. Every identity
// here still EXECUTES in the fold and its outcome is still asserted; what the roster changes is
// which outcome counts as agreement. A row that fails as enrolled is agreement. A row that is
// NOT enrolled and fails reds the build. A row that IS enrolled and PASSES also reds the build,
// naming itself and asking to be removed — so a fix is counted the moment it lands rather than
// being absorbed into a green run.
//
// That last arm is what makes this monotone. A skip list grows quietly and its members rot,
// because nothing observes them and nothing notices when they start working. This roster can
// only shrink under an honest run, and its length is the debt.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS. The floor cut replaced an evaluator that ran a selected subset per PR with one
// that folds the whole discovered roster through a single prepared subject. The replacement is
// faster end to end than the floor it replaces and runs strictly more rows, and it fails a
// population the old one did not — some because this branch broke them, some because the old
// floor never executed them. Holding the whole branch until every one is repaired keeps everyone
// on the slower, less complete floor in the meantime. Enrolling them keeps the new floor's
// verdict honest while they are worked.
//
// WHAT IT MUST NOT BECOME. A row belongs here only while someone is fixing it. It is not a
// disposition: it names no executing consumer other than this floor, it establishes no coverage,
// and an identity that sits here indefinitely is a defect nobody owns wearing a receipt. The
// roster is admissible because it is temporary, visible at identity grain, and self-emptying —
// remove any of those three and it is the skip list it is carefully not.
//
// THE PRESENT POPULATION IS NOT WRITTEN HERE, AND THAT IS THE CORRECTION RATHER THAN AN
// OMISSION. It is `floor_expected_red_roster()` -- the fold over the chunks below -- and that
// fold is the only authority for it. A transcribed total is a SECOND representation of a fact
// this module already computes (DESIGN 2/3): nothing derives from the sentence, so nothing
// refuses when it goes stale, and it goes stale on the roster's own removal path, which is
// designed to fire without anyone editing this header.
//
// It had already gone stale twice over, in the way that makes the class worth naming: this
// block carried TWO current-state totals at once -- `311` here and `306` immediately below --
// each correct when written, neither correct together, and the roster they both described had
// moved on from both. Two accounts of one population in one comment is the same defect as two
// authorities for one fact; the repair is to delete both rather than to reconcile them into a
// third number that will rot on the same schedule.
//
// WHAT SURVIVES, and why the distinction is not cosmetic: a DATED MEASUREMENT bound to a named
// run is a receipt -- it says what was observed on a tree that still exists, and it stays true
// forever because its subject is fixed. A BARE PRESENT-TENSE TOTAL is a claim about the live
// roster, and the live roster is exactly what this file already computes. So the derivations,
// the run ids, the 429/313 partition and the removal history below all remain untouched; only
// the two sentences asserting a current count are gone.
//
// The five rows added 2026-08-22 are the previously-INVISIBLE rows described at chunk_20 --
// they enter this roster from marker-less files rather than from a regression, which is a route
// this roster had not carried before, and that route is the reason they are called out here.
//
// The derivation below is a receipt and is unchanged: the declared rung drop took 111 out by
// relocation and run 32189201111 (PR #8460) observed 52 more PASSING (469 -> 361 -> 358 -> 306
// as of that run). Those 52
// reported STALE-QUARANTINE -- enrolled as expected-red and PASSED -- which is this roster's own
// removal path, not a roster edit: they pass because that branch closed two emitter roots (a
// builtin answering a call the program had already bound to a lexical binding, and a bare closure
// emitted where an arrow return was declared), so witnesses red against those defects now agree.
// That run named 57; the other 5 had already been removed on main by the time it landed. The count is NOT the whole population affected, and the rest is stated
// exactly here rather than left to the waves: 38 modules were relocated carrying 429 `test fn`
// declarations. THE PARTITION MUST BE DISJOINT, and an earlier revision of this block was not:
// it read "111 enrolled, 111 over-budget, 207 healthy", which sums to 429 only by counting the
// 102 rows that are BOTH enrolled here AND over budget on both sides of the sum. Enrolment and
// cost are independent axes, so the partition is taken on enrolment and the cost facts are
// stated inside each part:
//
//   111 enrolled here and removed (the 469 -> 358 delta), which the pre-quarantine run
//       32177951514 partitions exactly -- 469 = 367 held red + 97 budget-refused + 5 passed
//       over budget -- so the 111 are 9 held red, 97 budget-refused, and all 5 passed-over-budget.
//     5 unenrolled and budget-refused.
//
//   97 AND 102 ARE BOTH CORRECT AND NAME DIFFERENT SETS, which is what made the earlier
//   accounts read as contradictory; neither may be written as a bare `budget_refused`:
//     budget-interrupted identities, global                    102
//       enrolled here AND budget-interrupted                    97
//       unenrolled AND budget-interrupted                        5
//     enrolled here AND completed-over-budget (stale_quarantine)  5
//   313 passing when they were relocated, enrolled nowhere, counted by no number in this file.
//       NINE of those later tipped over the ceiling once wave 1 removed the slow head (8 in
//       32186963581, 1 straggler in 32193032348), so 304 have never been observed over budget.
//       THE EIGHT ARE 32186963581, not 32189985063, and the counters decide it without reading
//       any prose: 32186963581 failed=8 known_red_held=361, 32189985063 failed=1
//       known_red_held=358, 32193032348 failed=1 known_red_held=358. Only one run observed
//       eight. The wrong id survived three rewrites of this block including mine, while the
//       SECOND WAVE paragraph below named the right one the whole time -- one file, two
//       accounts of one population, which is why the run counters and not the neighbouring
//       sentence are the authority here.
//
//   111 + 5 + 313 = 429. Independently: `grep -c '^test fn'` over the 38 relocated modules is
//   also 429, and differencing the two floor runs gives the same split (below).
//
// THE PROBLEM POPULATION IS NOT ONE NUMBER EITHER, which is why several circulated at once:
// 107 rows were over budget or failing at quarantine time (102 budget-refused + 5 passed over
// budget), and 116 including the 9 that tipped afterwards. 102 is the interrupted rows alone.
//
// The figures above are checkable against the tree and the run counters, and they are stated
// because the earlier revision of this line said 361 while the roster carried 358, said 108
// where the delta is 111, and described its collateral as "roughly 200". A bounded population
// is the one thing a rung drop must not round: sharp-raven-273's monotone-shrink wall reads
// this carrier as its baseline, so a stale count here is load-bearing for work that is not
// mine. The 358/111 corrections are tidy-lark-471's external review; the disjointness and the
// per-part cost split are royal-hawk-392's.
//
// HISTORY: cut at 820 on tree 380c242c79a; 25 were removed after the first complete
// run on main (611fd027708) observed them PASSING and red the build naming themselves, which is the
// removal path working exactly as designed rather than a roster edit. 164 more were removed once
// prepared-floor scopes began binding bare helper names by precedence instead of corpus order, and
// 33 more after run 32162750949 observed them PASSING on the merge ref — the same removal path,
// twice more. EVERY removal here is an OBSERVED pass, never an inference: a row reported
// BUDGET-REFUSED is UNDECIDED and stays enrolled, because the floor's own diagnostic is right that
// removing it would not help when it is not passing either.
// The count is a fact about that exact tree and nothing else — this branch's diagnostic
// sensitivity is not monotone across trees, so these names may not be comparable to a count taken
// on main or on an earlier head. Each is enrolled at exact qualified-name grain, never by prefix,
// module, or directory, so enrolling one row can never silently enroll a neighbour.
//
// DECLARED RUNG DROP (DESIGN 4b), 2026-08-18, operator-directed, temporary.
//
// PREVIOUS RUNG: 469 enrolled identities, every one executing on the required floor each run.
// TEMPORARY RUNG: 361. One hundred and eight enrollments were removed here, not because any
// row was observed passing -- the ONLY removal reason this roster otherwise admits -- but
// because their modules were relocated to long/ homes and no longer execute at all.
//
// REASON: 102 witnesses could not finish inside required_floor_claim_budget_ms (1552ms), and
// reported across two channels that both exit nonzero (budget_refused, stale_quarantine). The
// floor could not deliver a verdict about the other 9,318 rows while they stood.
//
// BOUNDED POPULATION, both halves, because neither is visible to any mechanism in this tree:
//   108 enrollments removed here, being every identity in the 21 relocated modules. That is
//       NOT 102: exactly 102 are the cost rows (verified by identity join against main run
//       32177951514 -- all 102 removed, none missed), and SIX are enrolled rows that were
//       behaving exactly as enrolled (known_red_held) and leave only because they share a
//       module with a relocated row. Naming them, because a population described as 102 while
//       being 108 is not bounded at the grain it claims:
//         gunbc.test.claim.realization_sweep_test.witness_canonical_identities_join_own_enrolled_roster
//         v2.test.execution.add_arrow_eval_by_execution.add_arrow_eval_direct_runtime_holds
//         v2.test.execution.add_arrow_eval_by_execution.add_arrow_eval_generality_executes_holds
//         v2.test.execution.add_arrow_eval_by_execution.add_arrow_eval_lazy_arm_branch_suppression_holds
//         v2.test.execution.add_arrow_eval_by_execution.add_arrow_eval_produced_add_body_executes_holds
//         v2.test.execution.add_arrow_eval_by_execution.add_arrow_eval_source_driven_add_executes_holds
//       These six are a THIRD collateral class, distinct from the 78: those are unenrolled and
//       passing, these were enrolled and correctly red. Both leave for the same reason -- a
//       module is the unit of relocation -- and both return when their module does.
//    78 healthy, fast, PASSING witnesses that left the floor as collateral, because a module
//       is the unit of relocation and they share a file with a slow row. Worst offenders:
//       roadmap_page_witness 29, live_read_classification 17, add_arrow_eval_by_execution 13.
// Nothing refuses on their absence. Nothing counts them. That is why they are written here.
//
// A JOIN AGAINST THE DEFERRAL FREEZE, AND THE INFERENCE I DREW FROM IT WAS WRONG (recorded
// 2026-08-19, corrected the same day by still-wolf-510 against a live run). The join is real:
// this roster and gunbc.witness_deferral_freeze name the same identities in 38-40 places
// depending on the head. What I concluded from it -- that those rows can never leave, because
// nothing executes them, so the self-emptying rule can never fire -- IS FALSE, and it was an
// assumption about a mechanism I did not read.
//
// The required floor excludes by AUTHORED MODULE NAME via required_floor long_home_prefixes.
// It does not consult the freeze at all. The freeze is an ADMISSION standing -- whether a row
// may remain without an executing consumer -- and the floor gate tolerates
// LegacyFrozenPathDeferral; it never suppresses execution. So a frozen path and a discovered
// module are independent facts, and an identity can be both.
//
// MEASURED, on green main run 32201531653: planned=9022 executed=9022 passed=8664
// known_red_held=358 failed=0, and the intersection's own members appear in that run's output
// -- test.claim.ci_exclusion_proof.ci_exclusion_proof_sentinel reports FAILED in 13640ns, which
// is a row executing and being held exactly as enrolled. These rows are evaluated every run and
// can leave the moment they pass.
//
// SO THE DEFECT IS THE OTHER WAY ROUND, and it is smaller and mechanical: the freeze rows are
// STALE. They assert path-deferral for identities that have an executing consumer -- the
// required floor itself -- so the honest disposition is to retire those freeze rows with a
// receipt, not to find the roster rows a cadence. The construction wall still earns its place,
// because the intersection is a contradiction whichever way it is resolved and nothing detects
// it today.
//
// KEPT RATHER THAN QUIETLY REWRITTEN because the error is this document's own subject: I joined
// two carriers correctly and then read one carrier's meaning off its name instead of its
// mechanism, which is exactly the stale-citation class DESIGN section 3 names, committed while
// correcting three other people's counts in the same file.
//
// WHAT THIS IS NOT, stated because the shape is dangerous: this is NOT the self-emptying rule.
// Every other removal from this roster came from OBSERVING a pass. These rows were removed by
// relocation, and a roster that silently went 469 -> 361 is indistinguishable, to every
// mechanism in the tree, from the defect that dropped 197 identities on 2026-08-18 and went
// unnoticed for an hour. An empty roster now refuses; a shrinking one still does not.
//
// NOT A PRECEDENT, and this sentence exists because the next reader will see a contradiction
// that is not there. `required_floor` states that relocating a file does not discharge a budget
// refusal, and that remains true and unweakened: an AUTHOR moving their own over-budget witness
// to a long home deletes the coverage while retaining the source, which is the gunbc#7762
// specimen the 2026-08-04 admission ruling exists for. What happened here is a different act --
// an operator-ruled, bounded, declared rung-drop with a named population and a restoration
// trigger, which DESIGN 4b permits explicitly. The distinction is declaration and reversibility,
// never the directory. So this quarantine is not grounds to revert that comment or that refusal,
// and a relocation offered WITHOUT these five declared fields is still the unsanctioned move.
//
// SECOND WAVE, 2026-08-18, operator-directed at a 1000ms line, plus one measured straggler.
// The first wave quarantined what was RED. Run 32186963581 then proved both budget channels
// clear (stale_quarantine=0, budget_refused=0) and surfaced EIGHT rows that had been passing
// under the ceiling and tipped over it once the slow head was gone -- the marginal population,
// measured rather than predicted. Sixteen more modules leave here: the fifteen over 1000ms, and
// test.claim.e0308_mechanical_trio_test at 774ms, which is below the line but FAILED on that
// run, so it is quarantined on evidence rather than on threshold. Three more enrolled rows
// retire with them (roster 361 -> 358; then 358 -> 306 on the #8460 passes above), and further healthy siblings leave under the same
// module-grain rule as the 78 above -- counted exactly in the POPULATION block, never rounded. The ceiling is 1552ms and it was calibrated as
// measured-max-plus-one-quantum from a SINGLE 1527ms observation, so rows within a few hundred
// ms of it are not stably green on a shared runner; that is the real reason this wave exists.
//
// THIRD ROW, 2026-08-18: test.claim.emitted_lib_rs_module_declaration_witness_test, at 42545ms.
// This one is worth naming separately because of HOW it was missed. My cost table was built from
// a run where the 30s wall interrupted the longest rows, so every censored row reported its
// deadline rather than its cost -- 30000ms, not 42545ms. The table therefore under-reported
// exactly the rows that most needed quarantining, and this module fell outside a 1000ms cut it
// exceeds by 42x. The measurement was a lower bound and I read it as a value. Its 3 witnesses
// leave with it; no roster row named it, so the relocation left the roster at 358 (306 after the #8460 removals).
//
// THE SECOND DERIVATION, kept because a coverage-loss figure asserted once is exactly what this
// declaration exists to prevent. Differencing the two floor runs on main reproduces the split
// above from run counters alone, without reading the tree:
//   pre  32177951514 @59653f569e  planned=9420 passed=8951 known_red=367 stale_quarantine=5 budget_refused=102
//   post 32193032348 @6f3d374de0  planned=8997 passed=8638 known_red=358 stale_quarantine=0 budget_refused=0
//   passed 313 + known_red 9 + budget_refused 102 + passed-over-budget 5 = 429.
// The 6-identity gap against `planned` (9420 - 8997 = 423, not 429) is six identities that
// LANDED between the two runs, in the three commits separating those heads. It is not a
// discrepancy in this population, and it is stated so the next reader does not read 423 as the
// answer.
//
// WHAT IS STILL NOT DERIVED, named rather than left to be assumed: which INDIVIDUAL identities
// make up the 313. The figures above are exact in aggregate and unavailable at identity grain,
// because the floor reports per-identity outcomes only as log lines and a scraped log is not an
// oracle. Closing that needs the floor to emit an identity-grained receipt, not a better scrape.
// So this block is SUPPORTING EVIDENCE for that receipt, not a substitute for it.
//
// AND `budget_refused=0` ON THE POST RUN IS NOT THE POPULATION DISAPPEARING. It means the floor
// stopped executing it. The 116 identities that were ever observed over budget or failing are
// still owed a remeasurement, at identity grain, against a cause -- not a green counter.
//
// A COST FIGURE HERE IS NOT A PROPERTY OF ITS WITNESS WHEN AN UNREPORTED LAZY SHARED FILL IS IN
// PLAY, and this quarantine proved it by accident.
// test.claim.emitter_nested_refinement_cast_witness_test
// single_refinement_carrier_emits_no_unsupported_cast cost 746ms on the pre run and 45941ms on
// the post run, unchanged in itself; its sibling in the same file costs 763ms. The relocated
// modules included every earlier caller of `compile_dag_rust_emit_check`, so the one-time
// `build_module_path_index_from_witness_roots` construction relocated onto the first surviving
// caller and that row became main's only failure.
//
// THE LAW IS NARROW, and stating it wider was this note's own first error (operator ruling,
// 2026-08-18): a restoration receipt is confounded WHEN CHANGING THE POPULATION CHANGES WHICH
// CLAIM PAYS AN UNREPORTED LAZY SHARED FILL. It is NOT that every restoration is confounded
// until every subject is prepared. Preparation is the remedy for THIS class -- process-scoped
// lazy first-touch construction -- and #8470 already applied it to the concrete instance above
// by paying the module-path index in floor preparation. It is not the remedy for closure
// pollution, fixture/live-index coupling, repeated end-to-end crossings, duplicate coverage,
// true whole-tree censuses, or genuinely size-scaling algorithmic cost, which are separate
// classes with separate owning layers. The remaining members of the lazy-fill class are what
// #8464's process-scoped cold-fill inventory is for, and they are the only ones this paragraph
// speaks about.
//
// RESTORATION TRIGGER: the cost lane. Measured by three sessions independently on 2026-08-18,
// the cost is the EVALUATION CONTEXT, not the witnesses -- a row taking 71s on the floor runs
// in 70-85ms in isolation, and a 12s row in 31ms.
//
// ONE CLAUSE OF THAT PARAGRAPH IS REFUTED BY EXECUTION (quiet-bee-353, 2026-08-19, gunbc#8478,
// remote executor, release claim_batch). It read: "dag_arrow_lambda rebuilds a 6,230-line
// grammar once per witness for 3-line sources because each claim gets a fresh frame by design."
// Folding that file from nineteen grammar builds to two DID NOT PAY -- 210.9s for the two
// folded claims against ~208s for the fourteen they replaced -- so the fold was reverted and
// the file left byte-identical. Solving the terms out of those runs: a `dag_grammar()`
// construction is ~0.5s and a single three-line parse through the interpreted frontend is ~12s.
// The apparatus is not the cost; the parse is. The arrow row must therefore NOT be planned as a
// witness-side repair, and twelve seconds to parse three lines is a frontend cost-shape defect
// that is plausibly the real subject behind several rows in this quarantine rather than an
// evaluation-context tax.
//
// SO "no witness has yet been found that is genuinely slow" IS NO LONGER ESTABLISHED, and this
// line is corrected rather than deleted because the claim was load-bearing for how the whole
// lane was planned: it licensed treating every relocated row as an apparatus problem. What is
// still true is that the two measured extremes above are apparatus. What is not established is
// that the residue is. When a module's cost is repaired it
// returns to the floor WHOLE and its healthy siblings return with it, so the collateral
// dissolves with work already scheduled rather than needing its own cleanup. This declaration
// is deleted when the relocated population reaches zero.
//
// FIRST RESTORATION DECISION, 2026-08-19: NOT YET, and the module returns WHOLE when it does.
// Recorded here because the session that declared this trigger is archived, so the next reader
// would otherwise find a trigger with no one holding it and no account of what was already
// decided against.
//
// The evidence offered was dag_arrow_lambda_witness_test, the densest parse-bearing module in
// the relocated population: same instrument, same flags, same budget, merge base 2c65eeacf32
// against a sync-token change. 12 of its 14 rows go 12237-14143ms to 193-555ms, 24x to 69x; the
// module total goes 228167ms to 27876ms; rows under the 1552ms ceiling go 0 of 14 to 12 of 14.
// The BEFORE column is the finding rather than the AFTER: fourteen rows parsing fourteen
// DIFFERENT sources agreed within 14% of each other, which is not fourteen expensive witnesses,
// it is one defect wearing fourteen names.
//
// Refused on two grounds, neither of them doubt about that measurement.
//
// FIRST, the AFTER column was measured on an unmerged branch head. Restoring against it would
// baseline the roster on a tree that does not exist, and if that branch changes before it lands
// the restoration rests on numbers nothing can reproduce. The trigger is repaired cost, and
// cost is repaired when the repair is IN main.
//
// SECOND, and this one is the trigger's own words: a module "returns to the floor WHOLE". Two
// of the fourteen are still over budget -- the first row pays the surviving preparation, and
// pre_repair pays two because it builds a MODIFIED grammar and so legitimately owes a second.
// Restoring twelve would split one file across two homes and mint a second module whose only
// membership rule is a cost threshold, which is the directory deciding an admission question
// (the 2026-08-04 ruling this file already cites). The correct wait is for the first-row
// constant to fall, after which all fourteen clear together and the module moves once.
//
// AND THE MEASUREMENT THAT SHOULD PRECEDE THE NEXT ONE, for the other 37 modules rather than
// this one: at least nine of the 38 were free riders that merely touched a shared corpus fill
// first, so quarantining them relocated the bill to the next module instead of removing it --
// watched changing hands on one cache key at identity grain. Floor observation noise is
// RELATIVE and flat at 4-5%, p95 13.5%, so the audit question for a quarantine decision is
// whether its observation sat within ~13.5% of the ceiling. Both decision runs behind the
// second wave clear that test by a wide margin, at 27x and 30x the ceiling.

// The rows are carried in fixed-size chunks and flattened, because one Cons chain 820 deep is a
// parse-depth hazard, not because the chunk boundaries mean anything. They do not: a chunk is a
// slice of an alphabetical list and nothing reads it.
fn floor_expected_red_chunk_00() -> List<String> {
  Cons { head: "test.claim.ci_exclusion_proof.ci_exclusion_proof_sentinel", tail:     Empty {} }
}

fn floor_expected_red_chunk_01() -> List<String> {
       Empty {}
}

fn floor_expected_red_chunk_02() -> List<String> {
  Cons { head: "test.claim.exact_witness_admission_witness.both_red_expectations_are_known_red_but_only_one_expects_a_pre_verdict_refusal", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.exact_witness_admission_witness.no_batch_mixes_a_pre_verdict_row_with_an_assertion_false_row", tail: Empty {} } }
}

